  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Donald Trump's approval ratings are lower than \never. Viktor Orban just suffered a landslide  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defeat. Most Brits now think Brexit was a massive \nmistake, and most Europeans trust EU bureaucrats  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than their own elected governments. So, \nit kind of looks like right-wing populism is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapsing, right? It seems that people are \nfinally realizing that random tariff barrages  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "won't fix the American economy and that populists \nmay promise to drain the swamp, but they really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean replace the swamp and that leaving the EU \ndid not result in more money for healthcare. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "populist surge in the west is over. This is the \nend. Or is it? Actually, the experience of early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adopter populist countries shows us that this \nis extremely unlikely. For example, in Austria,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "populism collapsed in the early 2000s after Jörg \nHaider's freedom party government turned out to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a chaotic mess. But once again, although not \nin power, the Austrian Freedom Party got the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "votes in the last election. Similarly, in Italy, \nthe populist Berlusconi faced a crushing defeat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after it became clear his billionaire who will \nrescue the economy narrative was not working. Yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Berlusconi always came back. And even when he \nhimself did not, Italy is now again ruled by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "populist right-wing president. In fact, looking at \nthese graphs, we can see that the dynamic is often  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86000,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same. Once right-wing populism emerges, yes, \nit often suffers crushing setbacks due to chaotic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infighting, no economic progress, and the exposure \nof massive corruption, but it never really goes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away. So, right-wing populists may suffer \ncrushing defeats and often embarrassing ones,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105200,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but eventually they always bounce back, which \nraises the question, why does populism always keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bouncing back? Why is it not going away after all \nof those broken promises? As always on Money and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Macro, we've emerged ourselves into the relevant \nscientific literature and selected the four most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prevalent explanations. Narrative number one, it's \nthe economy stupid? Ordinary people have been left  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind. Narrative number two, right-wing populists \nhave a point. Mainstream politicians themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the problem. They've become out of touch \non cultural issues like immigration. Narrative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number three, people have just become more racist/ \nauthoritarian. And last but not least, narrative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number four, it's actually social media that's \nto blame. That is pushing people to political  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremes. In this video, we examine the evidence \nand counter evidence for each theory to determine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162000,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which of them has the most convincing explanation \nfor why right-wing populism keeps bouncing back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before solving the puzzle, we first need to \nmake sure we are all talking about the same thing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, what is right-wing populism actually? \nAnd how is it different from left-wing populism?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aren't these polar opposites? Why are we talking \nabout them in the same breath? Well, yes and no.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186480,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is something people often get wrong. Populism \nis not at all like socialism or like conservatism.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not an ideology. It's a way of talking about \npolitics a lens through which political conflict  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is framed. At its core, both left and right-wing \npopulism divides society into two opposing groups.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand, the hardworking people and on \nthe other, the corrupt elites. Populism is pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy to understand. Essentially, all problems in \nsociety are the fault of these corrupt elites. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference between left and right-wing populism \nthen is which elite group they frame as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem. On the left, populists target economic \nelites such as rich and the corporations, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brolicharchy of tremendous wealth, a government \nof the billionaire class by the billionaire class.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, right-wing populists target \na different kind of elite group, the cultural  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elites, such as \"The Brussels bureaucrats, the \nAmercian bureaucrats, and the Soros network...\" "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also academics or mainstream politicians \naccused of ignoring ordinary people's concerns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253200,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about immigration, crime, and cultural identity. \nDifferent enemies, same narrative. The system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been captured by elites which no longer \nrepresent the people and simple narratives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "often come with simple solutions. So if you \nbelieve that moneyed elites cause most problems,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you should vote left-wing populists into power \nso that they can tax their wealth away. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you believe immigration is damaging \nthe economy and eroding local culture,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you should back leaders who are not afraid to defy \ncultural elites and take massive actions to close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the borders. Especially the right-wing populist \nstory convinced a lot of people in the West in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the last couple of decades. But okay, back to the \npresent. Trump's remanufacturing America story is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "failing. Brexit is not making Britain rich and \nOrban just lost in a landslide. So why again do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305200,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we say right-wing populism will bounce back soon \nenough? Well, let's start with the economic story  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312880,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that should in theory empower left-wing populists. \nBut surprisingly in reality often empowers the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right. Narrative number one, people vote populist \nbecause they are economically left behind. Okay,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this map shows GDP per capita per German state. \nAnd this map shows where people mostly voted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the right-wing populist party AFD. This is \nnot a coincidence. When we look at leaders like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Marine Le Pen and Viktor Orban, we see that they \noften struggle to gain traction in thriving rich  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "urban centers, but they dominate the vote \nin rural and declining areas. By now, it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty well known that the hyper globalization \nperiod from the late 1970s to early 2000s created  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "winners and losers. Chinese workers were \nable to produce for the rest of the world,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massively improving their own living standards. \nMeanwhile, in the west, highly educated people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in big cities in rich countries were also able to \nexport services to more and more people globally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which translated into higher wages for them. \nBut take a look at this graph. The hourly wage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corrected for inflation for a US worker without \nhigher education is very similar today to what it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was in 1965. So while during the period of hyper \nglobalization the US economy kept growing only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the well-educated cultural and moneyed elites have \nactually seen their purchasing power go up while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many ordinary people were indeed left behind. \nIt's not hard to see how this can translate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into politics. If you feel the system no longer \nworks for you and the same political elites keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defending it. Of course you will start looking for \nalternatives and that is exactly what we see in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the data. Regions more exposed to Chinese import \ncompetition were more likely to support Brexit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Trump, and the AFD in Germany. Areas more exposed \nto automation tend to show stronger support for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421760,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "populist parties. However, while this evidence \nlooks convincing, it has three big problems.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, if we look at people in countries that \ntruly lost a lot of their income between 1985  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 2015, we would expect right-wing \npopulism to have surged in countries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Spain and Portugal. But actually, relatively \nfortunate and less unequal countries like Norway,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finland, Switzerland, and Austria have \nseen far bigger right-wing populist surges.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second problem with the explanation is \nonce we move from regions to individuals,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the story weakens. As research by political \nscience professor Larry Bartles shows, economic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disaection turns out to be a poor predictor \nof whether someone votes or identifies with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right-wing populism. It is far less informative \nthan attitudes towards immigration, trust in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "political institutions, or broader cultural \nvalues. In other words, yes, many people in left  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind areas vote for the populist right. But if \nyou want to actually predict whether someone will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support the populist right, you get much closer \nby asking what they think about immigration or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether they trust politicians than by asking them \nabout what they think about the economy. The third  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem is if populism is about regular working \nfolks wanting an equal share of the economic pie,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn't it make sense to vote for populists \nwho want to redistribute wealth, so those on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the left? However, if we look at the data, we \nsee the opposite. It's especially right-wing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "populism that is surging in the West as inequality \nincreased. So, could it be that people are fooled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the populist right to vote against their own \ninterest? That's a story that we hear a lot and it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be true but actually and it may shock some \nof you hearing this from me in this case maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economics is not everything. So it's time for this \neconomist here to step outside of his comfort zone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with narrative number two. Right-wing populism \nis actually about out of touch cultural elites.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay. The argument goes a little bit like this. No \nvoters are not confused. The real people who are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549760,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confused are the cultural elites. I really believe \nthat Westminster, the intellectuals, the liberal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557440,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elites just don't get it. Take a simple question. \nShould immigrants adapt to the customs of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564960,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "country that they move to? Large majorities of \nthe public say yes. This holds all across Europe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Britain, Denmark, Germany, and Sweden. But among \npolitical elites, only those in Denmark agree with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that view. On the other hand, in Germany, all \npoliticians except for the populist right, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yes, that even includes conservative politicians, \nare more liberal on immigration than the average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "voter? Or take the question, should race be taken \ninto account in college admissions? Only a third  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Americans agree with this view. Yet mainstream \nDemocrat leaders from Hillary Clinton to Joe Biden  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have consistently supported raceconscious policies \nand criticized court rulings against them. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disconnect between what the public wants and \nwhat politicians want on the other hand is what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "political scientists call a representation \ngap. As you can see here in this FT graph,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "politicians and voters are roughly aligned when \nit comes to economic issues like redistribution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and capitalism. Left-wing politicians are close \nto leftwing voters and right-wing politicians  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are close to right-wing voters. But when it \ncomes to topics like immigration and crime,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "politicians are on average far more to the left \nthan the average voter. So when the political  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "debate is dominated by economic issues like \ntaxes and redistribution, well in those cases,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mainstream parties are relatively safe because \nthey champion what voters actually want. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when cultural issues move to the center of the \ndebate, the dynamics change. If voters feel that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their basic cultural preferences are ignored \nor even worse, dismissed or talked down upon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that gap becomes a big opportunity for right-wing \npopulists to emerge. The clearest example of this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the 2015 European refugee crisis. As images \nof large numbers of asylum seekers and irregular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "border crossings dominated the news, immigrations \nshot up the list of public concerns. People did  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not necessarily purely react to immigration \nitself, but they did react strongly to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perception of chaos and loss of control. And when \nthat perception takes hold and politicians are not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reacting to it, then right-wing populists gain a \nbig advantage. Research by James Denison and Andre  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fer Gues shows that as immigration becomes more \nsalient, support for populist right-wing parties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rises right along with it. Not perfectly, not \neverywhere, but consistently enough to matter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, as citizens, we're like, \"Oh \nmy goodness, look at what's going on here. Our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "societies are changing dramatically. We're in the \nmidst of this economic stagnation where, you know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I feel like I have to compete for jobs. I have to \ncompete for public goods.\" And at the same time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traditional establishment parties are inviting in \ntons of immigrants, allowing huge asylum flows,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telling me that if I worry about this or I'm \nconcerned about this, I'm a racist or a xenophobe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, interestingly, when populist right-wingers say \nthat cultural elites like mainstream politicians  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are out of touch, data shows us that they are \ncorrect. Not when it comes to economics, but when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it comes to social issues like immigration and \ncrime. Yes, the general public is significantly to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the right of most politicians. But as we've seen, \nthere's only one exception. In Denmark, the Social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Democrats adopted stricter immigration policies \nthemselves, which coincided with a decline in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770800,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support for the populist right from over 20% to \njust below 10%. But okay, that's still 10%. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there must be more going on. But this was still a \ncrucial story. In many leftwing circles, though,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the diagnosis is very different. The problem they \nargue goes far beyond out of touch elites. No,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790880,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it points to the people. Narrative number \nthree, people are simply becoming more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "racist. And honestly, this is not super popular in \nacademic circles. This explanation, it's actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an argument that I often hear from friends, so \nI thought it was important to address it here.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nonetheless, many of them watch extreme takes \nfrom populist right-wingers and their supporters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They scroll through online comment sections and \ncome away thinking something genuinely dark is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happening. People are becoming evil. People are \nstigmatizing entire Muslim communities as violent.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Politicians are showing authoritarian tendencies. \nThere are conspiracies about Brussels bureaucrates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or George Soros erasing Western civilization. It \ncan feel like people are forgetting the lessons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the past, growing tired of democracy and \nbecoming more xenophobic and conspiratorial. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is it actually true? Are people really becoming \nless satisfied with democracy and more hostile to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immigrants? Well, according to political scientist \nLarry Bartles, the answer to that question is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clear. No. Absolutely not. Based on survey \nresponses from more than 300,000 Europeans, he  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finds that they have not become significantly more \nilliberal or more hostile to immigration over the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past two decades. In fact, support for European \nintegration has remained fairly stable throughout  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the 21st century. Level of trust in parliament and \npoliticians, satisfaction with democracy and even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attitudes towards immigration. They have remained \nbroadly stable over time and have even improved a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little. Of course, there are some exceptions. For \nexample, Spanish citizens became much more hostile  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to democracy after the massive economic crisis \nin 2008. However, satisfaction with democracy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been rising in Finland, Sweden, and Denmark \nat a time when the populist right became a major  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "player in those countries. So overall, no, it does \nnot appear that people are becoming more racist or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "authoritarian, but I do understand why more and \nmore people feel this way. The YouTube shorts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "algorithm sometimes recommends me these nice \nvideos of how US presidents used to interact with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one each other. I think Mr. Nixon is an effective \nleader of his party. The question before us is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which point of view and which party do we want to \nleave the United States? I agree with Senator K.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if we compare that to some of the stuff that \nTrump says these days, sleepy, crooked Joe Biden,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where I could stand in the middle of Fifth Avenue \nand shoot somebody and I wouldn't lose any voters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, it's like incredible. Then it's clear that \nsomething has changed. The public is willing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to accept far more uncivilized language. And \nactually, this is not just an American phenomena.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right-wing populist leaders everywhere use \nincreasingly inflammatory language. So why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955280,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does the public increasingly accept that type of \nbehavior if they themselves did not become more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "authoritarian? Well, this brings us to narrative \nnumber four. Blame social media. Social media is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "polarizing us. And there's really good evidence \nthat this is actually happening in two ways.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, as you can see here, traditional media like \nnewspapers and radio, well, they gave a voice to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyone, the hard left, the moderates, and the \nhard right. However, as you can see here, cable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984800,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TV is already quite a bit more biased towards \nboth extremes. And on social media, well, oh boy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the algorithms really love to serve us both hard \nleft and hard right content, while the moderate,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuanced, more boring voices are buried in the \nalgorithms. To explain this, psychologist J.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004880,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Vanbo describes four dark laws that drive online \nengagement. Negativity, extremism, us versus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them thinking, and moral emotional language. In \nother words, content that is more negative, more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme, more tribal, and more emotionally charged \nis more likely to grab attention and spread. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that expressing outrage, attacking the other \nside, or using words like disgusting or unjust  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or just super simple narratives will consistently \noutperform more balanced or nuanced views. So, if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are on social media a lot, it makes sense that \nyou will start believing that people are angrier,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more polarized, and more extreme than that they \nactually are. But it gets worse. The second way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that social media is polarizing us other than \namplifying extreme voices is by connecting extreme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "voices. You see, views that were once socially \ntaboo like I could have done things to her that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would have made your head spin used to remain on \nthe fringes. But nowadays, people holding these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "views can find each other instantly. When they \nrealize that they're not alone, then they become  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more confident to express those views in public. \nSo the internet did not necessarily create these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attitudes, but it did make them more visible, more \norganized, and more politically relevant. And this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can explain why many of my friends that spend a \nlot of time online feel that people on average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have become more extreme, even though research \nshows us that they did not. However, again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is not the full story. You see, cable TV and \nsocial media spread pretty evenly worldwide. Yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right-wing populism patterns vary drastically \nacross countries. Not every country has ended  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with Americanstyle media polarization. \nSo this brings us to our conclusion. Which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrative is the most convincing? Well, one \nthing it's clear, it's not narrative number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three. People are not becoming more racist \nor authoritarian over time. Anti-democratic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and anti-immigration sentiment did not just \nappear out of nowhere in the last decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's always been a slice of a population that \nfeels this way, which means there's always been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some baseline demand for right-wing populism. \nBut for the other narratives, given that country  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trajectories are so different, the different \nright-wing populist surges can probably best  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1142640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be explained by combining these three other \nnarratives. First, there was rising inequality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "austerity, the fallout from the financial crisis. \nAll of that shook people's trust in institutions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1156080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This could explain populist surges in the American \nMidwest or Eastern Europe. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1161840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "populist surges in relatively equal countries like \nNorway and Denmark probably have more to do with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1167680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a political disconnect on cultural issues. And \nfinally, the rise of social media and cable TV  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably acted like an amplifier, giving populist \nparties that represent 10 to 30% of the population  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1180320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the power to take over several countries. So yeah, \nall of these three explanations are supported by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the evidence. On their own though, there are \ntoo many contradictions to believe them. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taken together, it becomes clear that right-wing \npopulism will likely surge back again and again  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as inequality remains high. Politicians \ndo not listen to voter preferences on culture and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "immigration. And number three, as long as social \nmedia gives populist an algorithmic edge. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1209680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yeah, that's my take. What do you think? Let me \nknow in the comments below. And if you find this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1215040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "topic as fascinating as I do, I highly recommend \nthat you check out the excellent analysis by our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advertising sponsor, The Economists, which I \nalways check out for the research process of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "my videos. For this episode in particular, \nI learned a lot from their analysis on how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Danish social democrats have shifted \nright on immigration to counter the far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1236720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right. Or if you also care about macroeconomic \nstability, I encourage you to take a look at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1242320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this article where they explore how efforts \nto be fiscally responsible and reduce budget  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1247920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deficits can sometimes end up benefiting populist \nparties. And finally, for a broader overview, I'd  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strongly strongly recommend this article on what \npopulism actually means. As these examples show,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1260240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Economist delivers insights that help you \nsee the bigger picture and think for yourself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1265440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is why I almost always rely on their analysis \nfor my research and it's why I highly recommend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1271120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you subscribe and that's actually now \nmore interesting than ever given that they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1276480,
